# `distr::Skellam` object

The object form of the Skellam family: it carries the parameters, and offers the density, the distribution function, random draws, the cumulant generating function and the cumulants as methods.

## Signature

```cpp
distr::Skellam(lambda1, lambda2);
pluss::Series distr::Skellam::pdf(const pluss::Series& x) const;
pluss::Series distr::Skellam::logpdf(const pluss::Series& x) const;
pluss::Series distr::Skellam::cdf(const pluss::Series& x) const;
pluss::Series distr::Skellam::logcdf(const pluss::Series& x) const;
pluss::Series distr::Skellam::sample(std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes, pluss::Config cfg = {}, std::uint64_t seed = 0) const;
pluss::Series distr::Skellam::sample_like(const pluss::Series& like, std::uint64_t seed = 0) const;
pluss::Series distr::Skellam::cgf(const pluss::Series& t) const;
pluss::Series distr::Skellam::cumulant(std::int64_t order) const;
```

## What It Does

`distr::Skellam` holds the parameters of the [Skellam](../discrete/Skellam.md) distribution, so that they are written once and the same object is used for every evaluation. Its methods give the same numbers as the kind-based functions `distr::pdf(dst::Skellam, ...)` and the others, on a whole `Series` at once.

- `pdf`, `logpdf`, `cdf` and `logcdf` evaluate the mass function, the distribution function and their logarithms.
- `sample` draws a `Series` of the given shape; `sample_like` draws one with the shape and dtype of an existing `Series`. The seed fixes the stream.
- `cgf(t)` is the cumulant generating function `log E[exp(t X)]`, and `cumulant(order)` the cumulant of that order (the first is the mean, the second the variance).

The parameters of the family are described on the [Skellam](../discrete/Skellam.md) page.

## Parameters

`lambda1, lambda2`
: the two fields `lambda1` and `lambda2`, set by aggregate initialisation.

`const pluss::Series& x`
: The points at which to evaluate, a `Series` of any shape; the result has the same shape.

`std::initializer_list<std::int64_t> sizes`
: The shape of the array of random draws, for example `{n}` or `{rows, columns}`.

`pluss::Config cfg`
: The dtype and platform of the draws; the default is Float32 on the CPU, so request Float64 when precision matters.

`std::uint64_t seed`
: The seed of the stream. The same seed always gives the same draws; a seed of 0 is a fixed default, not a random one.

`const pluss::Series& like`
: A Series whose shape and dtype `sample_like` copies for the draws; its values are not used.

`const pluss::Series& t`
: The points at which to evaluate the cumulant generating function.

`std::int64_t order`
: The order of the cumulant: 1 is the mean, 2 the variance, 3 the third cumulant, and so on.

## Returns

`pdf`, `logpdf`, `cdf`, `logcdf` and `cgf` return a `Series` of the shape of their argument; `sample` and `sample_like` return the drawn `Series`; `cumulant` returns a one-element `Series`.

## Errors

- Parameters outside their valid range are refused when a method is called (`mu1` and `mu2` must be positive).

## Methods

- `pdf` -- the density (mass function) at each point.
- `logpdf` -- the logarithm of the density.
- `cdf` -- the distribution function.
- `logcdf` -- the logarithm of the distribution function.
- `sample` -- random draws of a given shape.
- `sample_like` -- random draws with the shape and dtype of an existing Series.
- `cgf` -- the cumulant generating function.
- `cumulant` -- the cumulant of a given order.

## Practical Examples

### Example 1

Example 1 uses the object form, `distr::Skellam`, which carries the parameters and offers the same functions as methods, plus `cumulant` for the moments.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);

  const distr::Skellam d{2.0, 1.5};
  const auto sample = d.sample({200000}, f64, 11ULL);
  const auto at = pluss::make_series({0.0}, f64);

  std::cout << "pdf at 0: " << as_f64(d.pdf(at)) << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf at 0: " << as_f64(d.cdf(at)) << '\n';
  const double m = as_f64(sample.mean());
  const double v = as_f64(((sample - m) * (sample - m)).mean());
  std::cout << "cumulant(1), the mean: " << as_f64(d.cumulant(1)) << "   mean of 200000 draws: " << m << '\n';
  std::cout << "cumulant(2), the variance: " << as_f64(d.cumulant(2)) << "   variance of the draws: " << v << '\n';
}
```

`cumulant(1)` and `cumulant(2)` are the exact mean (0.5) and variance (3.5) of the distribution, computed from the parameters without drawing anything; the mean (0.49578) and variance (3.49111) of 200000 draws land within sampling error of them (the standard error of the mean is about 0.00418).

### Example 2

Example 2 uses every evaluation method of the object `distr::Skellam`: the density (mass function), its logarithm, the distribution function and its logarithm, the cumulant generating function `cgf`, and the cumulants of orders 1 to 4.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);

  const distr::Skellam d{2.0, 1.5};
  const auto x = pluss::make_series({-4.0, -2.0, -1.0, 0.0}, f64);
  const auto t = pluss::make_series({0.1, 0.2, 0.3}, f64);

  std::cout << "x:";
  for (double v : x.pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "pmf:";
  for (double v : d.pdf(x).pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "logpmf:";
  for (double v : d.logpdf(x).pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "cdf:";
  for (double v : d.cdf(x).pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "logcdf:";
  for (double v : d.logcdf(x).pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';
  std::cout << "cgf at t = 0.1 0.2 0.3:";
  for (double v : d.cgf(t).pull<double>()) std::cout << ' ' << v;
  std::cout << '\n';

  std::cout << "cumulants 1 to 4:";
  for (int order = 1; order <= 4; ++order) std::cout << ' ' << as_f64(d.cumulant(order));
  std::cout << '\n';
}
```

`logpdf` and `logcdf` are the logarithms of the density and of the distribution function, computed directly, which is more accurate in the tails than taking the logarithm of a tiny number: at the first point exp(-4.48319) = 0.0113, the density 0.0113 printed above. The cumulants are 0.5, 3.5, 0.5, 3.5: the first is the mean, the second the variance, the third measures skewness (it is zero for a symmetric distribution), and the fourth the excess of the tails over a normal one. The cumulants are the Taylor coefficients of `cgf`: 0.1 κ1 + 0.1² κ2 / 2 + 0.1³ κ3 / 6 + 0.1⁴ κ4 / 24 = 0.0676 against `cgf(0.1)` = 0.0676.

### Example 3

Example 3 draws random values from the object: `sample_like` takes the shape and dtype of an existing Series, `sample` takes a shape, and the seed fixes the stream; a large sample reproduces the first two cumulants.

@@run@@
```cpp
#include <pluss/ma.h>

#include <iomanip>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

namespace distr = pluss::subsp::stats::distr;
namespace dst = distr::dst;

int main() {
  const auto f64 = pluss::Config{}.with_dtype(pluss::ScalarType::Float64);
  std::cout << std::fixed << std::setprecision(5);

  const distr::Skellam d{2.0, 1.5};

  // sample_like draws a Series with the shape and dtype of an existing one; the seed fixes the stream.
  const auto like = pluss::series::zeros({2, 3}, f64);
  const auto draws = d.sample_like(like, 11ULL);
  std::cout << "shape of the draws: " << draws.sizes()[0] << " x " << draws.sizes()[1] << "\n";

  const auto again = d.sample_like(like, 11ULL);
  std::cout << "same seed, same draws: " << (as_f64((draws - again).abs().max()) == 0.0 ? "yes" : "no") << "\n";
  const auto other = d.sample_like(like, 12ULL);
  std::cout << "other seed, other draws: " << (as_f64((draws - other).abs().max()) > 0.0 ? "yes" : "no") << "\n";

  // sample takes the shape directly; a large sample estimates the mean and the variance.
  const auto big = d.sample({200000}, f64, 5ULL);
  const double m = as_f64(big.mean());
  const double v = as_f64(((big - m) * (big - m)).mean());
  std::cout << "mean of 200000 draws " << m << " (cumulant(1) = " << as_f64(d.cumulant(1)) << ")\n";
  std::cout << "variance of 200000 draws " << v << " (cumulant(2) = " << as_f64(d.cumulant(2)) << ")\n";
}
```

